\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-350532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a0113cdf61e3bfbe63d30e1064ed1367e3e933afe27bca9cc7249993406af5b"} {"question" "q-350532" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-350532" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-350546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{({x}^{3}-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-350546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-12$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{({x}^{3}-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-12}\\frac{3}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-12}\\frac{3}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-12}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-12} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-12} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-12)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{12}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-350546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a0113cdf61e3bfbe63d30e1064ed1367e3e933afe27bca9cc7249993406af5b"} {"question" "q-350546" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-350546" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-152077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-152077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=1+2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+2x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{{u}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-152077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a0113cdf61e3bfbe63d30e1064ed1367e3e933afe27bca9cc7249993406af5b"} {"question" "q-152077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-152077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-152077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-350542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{(4t+3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-350542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=4t+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4t+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{(4t+3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{5}{2{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{5}{2{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-350542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a0113cdf61e3bfbe63d30e1064ed1367e3e933afe27bca9cc7249993406af5b"} {"question" "q-350542" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-350542" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-20884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-20884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=7x+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=7x+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{7{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{7{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{16}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{224}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-20884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a0113cdf61e3bfbe63d30e1064ed1367e3e933afe27bca9cc7249993406af5b"} {"question" "q-20884" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-20884" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{224}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{7}"] ["math" "0"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-20884" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{224}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-100495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-100495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}-1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}-1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-2$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{-8}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-100495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a0113cdf61e3bfbe63d30e1064ed1367e3e933afe27bca9cc7249993406af5b"} {"question" "q-100495" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-100495" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{24}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{8}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-100495" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{24}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-101735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z$\n- ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-101735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{z+3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a+3}+\\frac{1}{0+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals III is divergent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-101735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a0113cdf61e3bfbe63d30e1064ed1367e3e933afe27bca9cc7249993406af5b"} {"question" "q-101735" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-101735" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-101735" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-20700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-20700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 2 & \\infty  \\\\ u & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{a}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)] \\\\  & =\\infty -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-20700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a0113cdf61e3bfbe63d30e1064ed1367e3e933afe27bca9cc7249993406af5b"} {"question" "q-20700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-20700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)"] ["math" "{e}^{2}"] ["text" "The integral is divergent"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-20700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The integral is divergent"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-101738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t$\n- ${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-101738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{u-1}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a-1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(0-{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[4\\sqrt{x-2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-\\sqrt{3-2}) \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\nIn conclusion, only integrals II and III are divergent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-101738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a0113cdf61e3bfbe63d30e1064ed1367e3e933afe27bca9cc7249993406af5b"} {"question" "q-101738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-101738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-101738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-100643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-100643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2-3t}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{2+3}-\\sqrt{2-3a}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{2-3a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-100643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a0113cdf61e3bfbe63d30e1064ed1367e3e933afe27bca9cc7249993406af5b"} {"question" "q-100643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-100643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["text" "The integral is divergent"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-100643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The integral is divergent"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-6032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-6032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{10}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-1}]}_{10}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2\\sqrt{10-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-6) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|) \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-6032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a0113cdf61e3bfbe63d30e1064ed1367e3e933afe27bca9cc7249993406af5b"} {"question" "q-6032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-6032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "q-6032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a3d73c51146b85d3c689cbb12e17c81e754ed69e12ac978d6e26e0f09761645"} {"question" "e-511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac { 8}{t^5}  \\,\\textrm{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/example-511.json" "file_sha256" "1f7cd81c1ed1667d1bd40081579ea8a5bc9b56037d67966883560c7ca4ead6b3"} {"question" "e-511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-4}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-2(0-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/example-511.json" "file_sha256" "1f7cd81c1ed1667d1bd40081579ea8a5bc9b56037d67966883560c7ca4ead6b3"} {"question" "e-10717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/example-10717.json" "file_sha256" "73b2ed70acdc75bf93e60583e5221c525cdac18314d44f0e961006d6e8970487"} {"question" "e-10717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{1}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/example-10717.json" "file_sha256" "73b2ed70acdc75bf93e60583e5221c525cdac18314d44f0e961006d6e8970487"} {"question" "e-512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/example-512.json" "file_sha256" "f2a030f9621ba5e6759ccd432865242da0b2ef78f1f0ace804d31bcf3633d237"} {"question" "e-512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.  \n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/example-512.json" "file_sha256" "f2a030f9621ba5e6759ccd432865242da0b2ef78f1f0ace804d31bcf3633d237"} {"question" "e-513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x} }\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/example-513.json" "file_sha256" "52085e349980b4f06c3f55be95be4ba39a848f59cf174bbcdebacf9056023f40"} {"question" "e-513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[2{x}^{1/2}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\sqrt{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{b}-1] \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\n- For the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{1+x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{1-2}+\\frac{1}{1+a}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{1+a}+1] \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\n- For the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007645/example-513.json" "file_sha256" "52085e349980b4f06c3f55be95be4ba39a848f59cf174bbcdebacf9056023f40"}] "usage" {"q-20884" [] "q-152777" [] "q-5962" [] "e-10717" [] "e-512" [] "e-511" [] "q-100495" [] "q-118619" [] "e-513" [] "q-6032" []} "basis" 1136 "retractions" [[17592186083453 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\infty}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x^{9}}}dx$?"] [17592186083736 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\infty}\\frac{3}{θ^{3}}dθ$?"] [17592186083409 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x}{(1 + x^{2})^{2}}dx$."] [17592186083521 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{- \\infty}^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x + 3)^{3}}dx$."] [17592186083388 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{- \\infty}^{-1}\\frac{3x^{2}}{(x^{3} - 1)^{4}}dx$."] [17592186083744 :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{10}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}dx$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{- \\infty}^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}dx$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{x^{2}}dx$"] [17592186082918 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac {8}{t^5} \\,\\textrm{d}t$."] [17592186082918 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{2}^{\\infty}\\frac{8}{t^{5}}dt &= \\lim_{a \\to \\infty}\\int_{2}^{a}\\frac{8}{t^{5}}dt \\\\\n&= 8\\lim_{a \\to \\infty}\\int_{2}^{a}t^{-5}dt \\\\\n&= 8\\lim_{a \\to \\infty}[- \\frac{1}{4}t^{-4}]_{2}^{a} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot \\lim_{a \\to \\infty}[\\frac{1}{t^{4}}]_{2}^{a} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot \\lim_{a \\to \\infty}(\\frac{1}{a^{4}} - \\frac{1}{2^{4}}) \\\\\n&=-2(0 - \\frac{1}{16}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186082808 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186082808 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute\n\n$$\nu=x+1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty$, the table for the limits of integration using the rule\n\n$$\nu=x+1\n$$\n\nis as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{(x + 1)^{2}}dx &= \\int_{1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{\\infty}u^{-2}du \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to \\infty}\\int_{1}^{a}u^{-2}du \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to \\infty}[- u^{-1}]_{1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\lim_{a \\to \\infty}[\\frac{1}{u}]_{1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot \\lim_{a \\to \\infty}[\\frac{1}{a} - \\frac{1}{1}] \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot (0 - 1) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186082919 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186082919 :question/worked-solution "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.\n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty$, the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x =\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{- \\infty}^{0}\\frac{1}{(x - 4)^{2}}dx &= \\int_{- \\infty}^{-4}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\int_{- \\infty}^{-4}u^{-2}du \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to - \\infty}\\int_{a}^{-4}u^{-2}du \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to - \\infty}[- u^{-1}]_{a}^{-4} \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot \\lim_{a \\to - \\infty}[\\frac{1}{u}]_{a}^{-4} \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot \\lim_{a \\to - \\infty}[\\frac{1}{(-4)} - \\frac{1}{a}] \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot \\lim_{a \\to - \\infty}(-\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{a}) \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186082920 :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$"] [17592186082920 :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n$∫_{1}^{∞}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}dx|= lim_(b → ∞)∫_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}dx; = lim_(b → ∞)∫_{1}^{b}x^{-1/2}dx; = lim_(b → ∞)[2x^{1/2}]_{1}^{b}; = 2lim_(b → ∞)[\\sqrt{x}]_{1}^{b}; = 2lim_(b → ∞)[\\sqrt{b} - 1]; = ∞$,\nso the integral diverges.\n- For the second integral, we have\n$∫_{- ∞}^{-2}(1)/((1 + x)^{2})dx|= lim_(a → - ∞)∫_{a}^{-2}(1)/((1 + x)^{2})dx; = lim_(a → - ∞)[- \\frac{1}{1 + x}]_{a}^{-2}; = lim_(a → - ∞)[- \\frac{1}{1 - 2} + \\frac{1}{1 + a}]; = lim_(a → - ∞)[\\frac{1}{1 + a} + 1]; = 1$,\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n- For the third integral, we have\n$∫_{1}^{∞}\\frac{1}{x^{3}}dx|= lim_(b → ∞)∫_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{x^{3}}dx; = lim_(b → ∞)∫_{1}^{b}x^{-3}dx; = lim_(b → ∞)[- \\frac{1}{2x^{2}}]_{1}^{b}; = lim_(b → ∞)[- \\frac{1}{2b^{2}} + (1)/(2(1^{2}))]; = lim_(b → ∞)[\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2b^{2}}]; = \\frac{1}{2} - 0; = \\frac{1}{2}$,\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186079659 17592186079660 17592186079661 17592186079662 17592186079663 17592186079785 17592186079786 17592186079787 17592186079788 17592186079789 17592186080049 17592186080050 17592186080051 17592186080052 17592186080053 17592186080625 17592186080626 17592186080627 17592186080628 17592186080629 17592186081783 17592186081784 17592186081785 17592186081786 17592186081787 17592186081831 17592186081832 17592186081833 17592186081834 17592186081835] "immutable_fields" [[17592186079658 :answer-field/correct] [17592186079658 :answer-field/id] [17592186079658 :answer-field/key] [17592186079658 :answer-field/type] [17592186079784 :answer-field/correct] [17592186079784 :answer-field/id] [17592186079784 :answer-field/key] [17592186079784 :answer-field/type] [17592186080048 :answer-field/correct] [17592186080048 :answer-field/id] [17592186080048 :answer-field/key] [17592186080048 :answer-field/type] [17592186080624 :answer-field/correct] [17592186080624 :answer-field/id] [17592186080624 :answer-field/key] [17592186080624 :answer-field/type] [17592186081782 :answer-field/correct] [17592186081782 :answer-field/id] [17592186081782 :answer-field/key] [17592186081782 :answer-field/type] [17592186081830 :answer-field/correct] [17592186081830 :answer-field/id] [17592186081830 :answer-field/key] [17592186081830 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
